WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.339
An image is sampled spatial data. High-frequency structure can produce the same stored samples as a

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:14.479
different lower-frequency pattern, so it becomes impossible to recover the original signal uniquely from those samples

00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:16.710
alone. This ambiguity is aliasing.

00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:23.053
The frequency is cycles per unit time or distance. The sampling rate uses the matching units.

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:29.781
Sampling keeps a sequence of values and discards what happened between them. Different continuous signals can

00:00:29.781 --> 00:00:30.947
share this sequence.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:37.671
Will frequencies four and eight give different sampled cosine values? Pause here and make a prediction

00:00:37.671 --> 00:00:38.447
before continuing.

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:47.210
For this cosine, frequencies mirrored around half the sampling rate have identical samples. This apparent frequency

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:54.854
cannot identify the original one uniquely. For our starting inputs, Original frequency: 4. Apparent frequency: 4.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:56.287
Nyquist limit: 6.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:04.909
Before reducing sampling density, suppress frequencies above the new limit. Interpolation alone cannot recover structure already

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:06.430
aliased by sampling.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:14.553
Resizing is a signal-processing operation. A sharper-looking resized image need not preserve the original information.

